Falsifies `machine_discovery::framebuffer::verification::every_hex_field_the_loader_writes_is_one_the_kernel_reads_back`.

The defect: `parse_hex` refuses seventeen digits instead of refusing more than
sixteen, so it also refuses the sixteen-digit values `write_hex` can emit.

Sixteen is `u64::MAX`'s width in hex, and a limit written as "more than sixteen is
not a u64" and one written as "sixteen is the last one that fits" are one character
apart. The reader and the writer are forty lines apart and neither mentions the
other.

The failure is silent in the direction that matters. `Framebuffer::parse` is
documented to read a malformed token **as no screen at all**, deliberately, so that
a display description the kernel could have done without does not stop the boot. So
a reader one digit short of the writer reports nothing: the machine comes up with a
black screen, and the UART, where there is one, is the only way to find out why. A
base above 2^60 is not hypothetical on a machine whose firmware relocated its own
windows, which is what xenon does.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(parse_hex(text), Some(value))`.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
@@ -284,7 +284,7 @@ fn write_decimal(value: u32, out: &mut [u8; 10]) -> usize {
 /// line would write. A bare number is refused rather than guessed at.
 fn parse_hex(text: &str) -> Option<u64> {
     let digits = text.strip_prefix("0x")?;
-    if digits.is_empty() || digits.len() > 16 {
+    if digits.is_empty() || digits.len() > 15 {
         return None;
     }
     let mut value = 0u64;
